<%# The classroom form, for both #new and #edit.

    Rebuilt. What was wrong with it, measured rather than asserted:

    - **Five elements shared `id="classroom_grade_ids_"`** - the hidden field and all four grade
      checkboxes - because `check_box_tag "classroom[grade_ids][]"` derives one id from the name. Invalid
      HTML, and any `for=` or `aria-*` reference resolves to whichever came first.
    - **The "Grades" label labelled nothing.** `form.label :grade_ids` emits `for="classroom_grade_ids"`,
      and no element has that id - the checkboxes end in an underscore. The group had no accessible name
      at all. It is a `<fieldset>` with a `<legend>` now, which is what a group of checkboxes is, and what
      design.md expects for a hand-placed group.
    - **Both option lists were `max-h-60 overflow-y-auto` boxes with a slate-50 fill** around content that
      trivially fits: four grades and, in this database, one teacher. A scroll container that can never
      scroll, and a tinted panel inside what is now a card - the same "conditional affordance with no
      condition" shape recorded elsewhere here.
    - **Two HTML comments** shipped to the browser. A view comment belongs in an ERB comment tag, which
      the server strips; an HTML one is sent and the reader can read it.
    - The form sat on **no surface** while students#new and #edit and every admin form use `.tw-card`.
    - `my-5` on each group, so the first and last field carried outer margin the card then doubled.

    School, year and the teacher assignment are admin-only, matching ClassroomPolicy's permitted
    attributes. A teacher who saw these would be filling in fields whose values are dropped on submit,
    which is worse than not offering them: the page would look like it saved and quietly would not. The
    check is the same policy the parameter filter uses, so the two cannot drift apart. %>
<%# **One classroom form, for both halves of the product.**

    `/classrooms/new` and `/admin/classrooms/new` were two different forms for one model, and they had
    drifted into two different products: this one asks for a school and a year and offers the teacher
    assignment; the admin one asked for a single school year, offered no teachers, wrapped every field in a
    `mb-6` and led with an `h3` restating the page title. An admin creating a classroom got different
    fields depending on which URL they came in through.

    They render the same partial now, so the two cannot diverge again. What varies is what has to vary: the
    URL it posts to and where Cancel goes. Everything else - which fields exist, in what order, with what
    validation - is one decision made once. %>
<% admin_fields = policy(classroom).permitted_attributes.include?(:school_id) %>
<% url = local_assigns.fetch(:url, nil) %>
<% cancel_path = local_assigns.fetch(:cancel_path) { classroom.persisted? ? classroom : classrooms_path } %>

<%= form_with(model: classroom, url: url, builder: Ui::FormBuilder, class: "contents") do |form| %>
  <%= render "shared/form_errors", record: classroom, subject: "classroom" %>

  <%# No `mt-*`. It carried `mt-4`, which did nothing in the ordinary case: adjacent vertical margins collapse
      to the larger, and the page header's own `mb-6` is bigger - measured, the gap was 24px with it and 24px
      without. An inert declaration is worse than none, because it reads as load-bearing and it *became*
      load-bearing the moment an error summary appeared between the two, giving that state a 16px gap where
      the rest of the page uses 24. The summary spaces itself now. %>
  <div class="tw-card p-5">
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <%= form.text_field :name,
                          label: "Name",
                          required: true,
                          hint: "What a teacher and their students will see it called." %>

      <%# **School** and **Year**, which are what `SchoolYearFields` accepts - the model resolves the pair
          into a SchoolYear on validation. The view used to reach through `classroom.school_year.school.id`
          to preselect each one; the model's readers do that now, so `form.select` finds its own value.

          The labels match the attributes they validate. "School name" here produced "School can't be
          blank" in the summary, naming something the reader could not find on the page - which is the
          whole reason the validation moved onto these two fields. %>
      <% if admin_fields %>
        <%= form.select :school_id,
                        options_from_collection_for_select(@schools, :id, :name, classroom.school_id),
                        { label: "School", prompt: "Select a school" },
                        { required: true } %>

        <%= form.select :year_id,
                        options_from_collection_for_select(@years, :id, :name, classroom.year_id),
                        { label: "Year", prompt: "Select a year" },
                        { required: true } %>
      <% end %>

      <%# The builder renders the fieldset, the legend, the hint under it and the group's error - all of
          which this did by hand, because the builder used to emit a `<label>` over a group with no single
          control to point at. That fix lives in `Ui::FormBuilder` now, so every group in the app gets it
          rather than the one form that was rebuilt.

          `Classroom::GRADE_RANGE`, not `Grade.all`: the admin half queried the latter straight from its
          view, which is how the two forms came to offer different grades. %>
      <%= form.collection_check_boxes :grade_ids,
                                      Grade.where(level: Classroom::GRADE_RANGE).order(:level),
                                      :id, :name,
                                      label: "Grades",
                                      required: true,
                                      errors_on: :grades,
                                      hint: "Select at least one grade that applies to this classroom." %>

      <% if admin_fields %>
        <%# Checkboxes, and no avatars.

            **The control is right for the volume.** A checkbox list is what GOV.UK, Polaris and Primer all
            use for a short, fully known set of options. Beyond roughly ten it should become a searchable
            multi-select with chips - GitHub's assignees picker, Linear's, Jira's - because a list you have
            to scroll to find a name in is a list you should be able to type into. That threshold is the
            trigger, not a redesign for its own sake.

            **The avatar is gone.** A 32px disc containing one letter makes nobody recognisable: two
            teachers whose names begin with T get the same disc, so it identified no one while taking the
            widest column in the row. The name and the email do the identifying, and the email is what
            disambiguates - which is why the label is a callable rendering two lines rather than a single
            `display_name`. %>
        <%= form.collection_check_boxes :teacher_ids, @teachers, :id, teacher_option_label,
                                        label: "Teachers",
                                        errors_on: :teachers,
                                        hint: "Teachers you select can see this classroom and edit its " \
                                              "grades and trading setting." %>
      <% end %>

      <%# **Last in the form**, after what the classroom *is* and who can see it.

          It is the only field here with a runtime effect on students, it is optional with a default, and it
          is the one setting that also has its own control on the classroom page - so in a form it is "and
          one more thing", not part of the classroom's identity. Required identity first, access next,
          behaviour last is the order GOV.UK and Polaris both use when fields are grouped on one page.

          A single checkbox is the right control and not a switch: GOV.UK reserves a lone checkbox for
          opting in, which is what this is, and a switch implies the change takes effect as you flip it -
          which it does on the classroom page, and does not here, where nothing happens until you save. %>
      <%= form.boolean_field :trading_enabled,
                             label: "Enable trading",
                             hint: "Students in this classroom can place buy and sell orders." %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# Leading edge, primary first, on the page background below the card - design.md's form-actions rule.
      The submit label is explicit: a bare `form.submit` lets Rails generate "Create Classroom" from the
      model name, which is Title Case and invisible to any copy sweep, since the string exists nowhere in
      the source.

      Cancel replaces the pair of `tw-btn-secondary` links that used to sit under the form - "View
      classroom" and "Back to classrooms" - which put three actions on the page in two groups with the
      primary orphaned above them. Going back is one action, and on #edit it goes where Cancel goes. %>
  <div class="tw-form-actions mt-6">
    <%= form.submit classroom.persisted? ? "Update classroom" : "Create classroom", class: "tw-btn-primary" %>
    <%= link_to "Cancel", cancel_path, class: "tw-btn-secondary" %>
  </div>
<% end %>
